## an8.30
an8.30:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Bhaggas at Crocodile’s Bellow, in the deer park at Bhesakaḷā’s Wood. | T: Once the Blessed One was staying among the Bhaggas in the Deer Park at Bhesakaḷā Forest, near Crocodile Haunt.
an8.30:1.2 | S: And at that time Venerable Anuruddha was staying in the land of the Cetīs in the Park of the East-Raftered Hall. | T: And at that time Ven. Anuruddha was living among the Cetis in the Eastern Bamboo Park.
an8.30:1.3 | S: Then as Anuruddha was in private retreat this thought came to his mind: | T: Then, as he was alone in seclusion, this line of thinking arose in Ven. Anuruddha’s awareness:
an8.30:1.4 | S: “This teaching is for those of few wishes, not those of many wishes. | T: “This Dhamma is for one who is modest, not for one who is self-aggrandizing.
an8.30:1.5 | S: It’s for the contented, not those who lack contentment. | T: This Dhamma is for one who is content, not for one who is discontent.
an8.30:1.6 | S: It’s for the secluded, not those who enjoy company. | T: This Dhamma is for one who is reclusive, not for one who is entangled.
an8.30:1.7 | S: It’s for the energetic, not the lazy. | T: This Dhamma is for one whose persistence is aroused, not for one who is lazy.
an8.30:1.8 | S: It’s for the mindful, not the unmindful. | T: This Dhamma is for one whose mindfulness is established, not for one whose mindfulness is confused.
an8.30:1.9 | S: It’s for those with immersion, not those without immersion. | T: This Dhamma is for one whose mind is concentrated, not for one whose mind is unconcentrated.
an8.30:1.10 | S: It’s for the wise, not the witless.” | T: This Dhamma is for one endowed with discernment, not for whose discernment is weak.”
an8.30:2.1 | S: Then the Buddha knew Anuruddha’s train of thought. As easily as a strong person would extend or contract their arm, he vanished from the deer park at Bhesakaḷā’s Wood in the land of the Bhaggas and reappeared in front of Anuruddha in the Park of the East-Raftered Hall in the land of the Cetīs, | T: Then the Blessed One, realizing with his awareness the line of thinking in Ven. Anuruddha’s awareness—just as a strong man might extend his flexed arm or flex his extended arm—disappeared from among the Bhaggas in the Deer Park at Bhesakaḷā Forest, near Crocodile Haunt, and re-appeared among the Cetis in the Eastern Bamboo Park, right in front of Ven. Anuruddha.
an8.30:2.2 | S: and sat down on the seat spread out. | T: There he sat down on a prepared seat.
an8.30:2.3 | S: Anuruddha bowed to the Buddha and sat down to one side. | T: As for Ven. Anuruddha, having bowed down to the Blessed One, he sat to one side.
an8.30:2.4 | S: The Buddha said to him: | T: As he was sitting there the Blessed One said to him,
an8.30:3.1 | S: “Good, good, Anuruddha! | T: “Good, Anuruddha, very good.
an8.30:3.2 | S: It’s good that you reflect on these thoughts of a great man: | T: It’s good that you think these thoughts of a great person:
an8.30:3.3 | S: ‘This teaching is for those of few wishes, not those of many wishes. | T: ‘This Dhamma is for one who is modest, not for one who is self-aggrandizing.
an8.30:3.4 | S: It’s for the contented, not those who lack contentment. | T: This Dhamma is for one who is content, not for one who is discontent.
an8.30:3.5 | S: It’s for the secluded, not those who enjoy company. | T: This Dhamma is for one who is reclusive, not for one who is entangled.
an8.30:3.6 | S: It’s for the energetic, not the lazy. | T: This Dhamma is for one whose persistence is aroused, not for one who is lazy.
an8.30:3.7 | S: It’s for the mindful, not the unmindful. | T: This Dhamma is for one whose mindfulness is established, not for one whose mindfulness is confused.
an8.30:3.8 | S: It’s for those with immersion, not those without immersion. | T: This Dhamma is for one whose mind is concentrated, not for one whose mind is unconcentrated.
an8.30:3.9 | S: It’s for the wise, not the witless.’ | T: This Dhamma is for one endowed with discernment, not for one whose discernment is weak.’
an8.30:3.10 | S: Well then, Anuruddha, you should also reflect on the following eighth thought of a great man: | T: Now then, Anuruddha, think the eighth thought of a great person:
an8.30:3.11 | S: ‘This teaching is for those who don’t enjoy proliferating and don’t like to proliferate, not for those who enjoy proliferating and like to proliferate.’ | T: ‘This Dhamma is for one who enjoys non-objectification, who delights in non-objectification, not for one who enjoys & delights in objectification.’
an8.30:4.1 | S: First you’ll reflect on these eight thoughts of a great man. Then as far as you want, quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, you’ll enter and remain in the first absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of seclusion, while placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: “Anuruddha, when you think these eight thoughts of a great person, then—whenever you want—quite secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities, you will enter & remain in the first jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation.
an8.30:5.1 | S: You’ll enter and remain in the second absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of immersion, with internal clarity and mind at one, without placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: When you think these eight thoughts of a great person, then—whenever you want—with the stilling of directed thoughts & evaluations, you will enter & remain in the second jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of concentration, unification of awareness free from directed thought & evaluation—internal assurance.…
an8.30:6.1 | S: You’ll enter and remain in the third absorption, where you’ll meditate with equanimity, mindful and aware, experiencing for themselves the bliss of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous and mindful, one meditates in bliss.’ | T: with the fading of rapture, you will remain equanimous, mindful, & alert, and sense pleasure with the body. You will enter & remain in the third jhāna, of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous & mindful, he has a pleasant abiding.’
an8.30:7.1 | S: With the giving up of pleasure and pain and the disappearance of former happiness and sadness, you’ll enter and remain in the fourth absorption, without pleasure or pain, with pure equanimity and mindfulness. | T: When you think these eight thoughts of a great person, then—whenever you want—with the abandoning of pleasure & pain, as with the earlier disappearance of elation & distress, you will enter & remain in the fourth jhāna: purity of equanimity & mindfulness, neither pleasure nor pain.
an8.30:8.1 | S: First you’ll reflect on these eight thoughts of a great man, and you’ll get the four absorptions—blissful meditations in this life that belong to the higher mind—when you want, without trouble or difficulty. Then as you live contented, your rag robe will seem to you like a chest full of garments of different colors seems to a householder or householder’s child. | T: “Now, when you think these eight thoughts of a great person and become a person who can attain at will, without trouble or difficulty, these four jhānas—heightened mental states providing a pleasant abiding in the here & now—then your robe of cast-off rags will seem to you to be just like the clothes chest of a householder or householder’s son, full of clothes of many colors.
an8.30:8.2 | S: It will be for your enjoyment, relief, and comfort, and for alighting upon extinguishment. | T: As you live contented, it will serve for your delight, for a comfortable abiding, for non-agitation, & for alighting on unbinding.
an8.30:9.1 | S: As you live contented, your scraps of almsfood will seem to you like boiled fine rice with the dark grains picked out, served with many soups and sauces seems to a householder or householder’s child. | T: “When you think these eight thoughts of a great person and become a person who can attain at will, without trouble or difficulty, these four jhānas—heightened mental states providing a pleasant abiding in the here & now—then your meal of almsfood will seem to you to be just like the rice & wheat of a householder or householder’s son, cleaned of black grains, and served with a variety of sauces & seasonings.…
