an8.30:23.1 | S: “Knowing my thoughts, | T: Knowing my thoughts,
an8.30:23.2 | S: the supreme Teacher in the world | T: the Teacher, unexcelled in the cosmos,
an8.30:23.3 | S: came to me in a mind-made body, | T: came to me through his power in a body made of mind.
an8.30:23.4 | S: using his psychic power. | T: (none)
an8.30:24.1 | S: He taught me more | T: He taught in line with my thoughts,
an8.30:24.2 | S: than I had thought of. | T: and then further.
an8.30:24.3 | S: The Buddha who loves non-proliferation | T: The Buddha, delighting in non-objectification,
an8.30:24.4 | S: taught me non-proliferation. | T: taught non-objectification.
an8.30:25.1 | S: Understanding that teaching, | T: Knowing his Dhamma,
an8.30:25.2 | S: I happily did his bidding. | T: I kept delighting in his bidding.
an8.30:25.3 | S: I’ve attained the three knowledges, | T: The three knowledges have been attained;
an8.30:25.4 | S: and have fulfilled the Buddha’s instructions.” | T: the Buddha’s bidding, done.
an8.30:25.5 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)
an8.30:25.6 | P: Gahapativaggo tatiyo. | T: (none)
an8.30:26.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
an8.30:26.1 | P: Dve uggā dve ca hatthakā, | T: (none)
an8.30:26.2 | P: mahānāmena jīvako; | T: (none)
an8.30:26.3 | P: Dve balā akkhaṇā vuttā, | T: (none)
an8.30:26.4 | P: anuruddhena te dasāti. | T: (none)

## an6.46
an6.46:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: (none)
an6.46:1.2 | S: At one time Venerable Mahācunda was staying in the land of the Cetis at Sahajāti. | T: On one occasion Ven. Mahā Cunda was staying among the Cetis in Sañjāti.
an6.46:1.3 | S: There he addressed the mendicants: | T: There he addressed the monks,
an6.46:1.4 | S: “Reverends, mendicants!” | T: “Friend monks!”
an6.46:1.5 | S: “Reverend,” they replied. | T: “Yes, friend,” the monks responded to him.
an6.46:1.6 | S: Venerable Mahācunda said this: | T: Ven. Mahā Cunda said,
an6.46:2.1 | S: “Take a case where mendicants who practice discernment of principles rebuke mendicants who practice absorption: | T: “Friends, there is the case where Dhamma-devotee monks [those devoted to memorizing and analyzing the Dhamma] disparage jhāna monks,
an6.46:2.2 | S: ‘They say, “We practice absorption meditation! We practice absorption meditation!” And they meditate and concentrate and contemplate and ruminate. | T: saying, ‘These people are absorbed and besorbed in jhāna, saying, “We are absorbed, we are absorbed.”
an6.46:2.3 | S: Why do they practice absorption meditation? In what way do they practice absorption meditation? How do they practice absorption meditation?’ | T: But why, indeed, are they absorbed? For what purpose are they absorbed? How are they absorbed?’
an6.46:2.4 | S: In this case the mendicants who practice discernment of principles are not inspired, and the mendicants who practice absorption are not inspired. And they’re not acting for the welfare and happiness of the people, for the people, for the benefit, welfare, and happiness of gods and humans. | T: In that, the Dhamma-devotee monks do not shine brightly, and the jhāna monks do not shine brightly. That is not practicing for the welfare of the masses, for the happiness of the masses, for the good of the masses, nor for the welfare & happiness of devas & human beings.
an6.46:3.1 | S: Now, take a case where mendicants who practice absorption meditation rebuke mendicants who practice discernment of principles: | T: “And further, there is the case where jhāna monks disparage Dhamma-devotee monks,
an6.46:3.2 | S: ‘They say, “We practice discernment of principles! We practice discernment of principles!” But they’re haughty, insolent, fickle, scurrilous, loose-tongued, unmindful, lacking situational awareness and immersion, with straying minds and undisciplined faculties. | T: saying, ‘These people say, “We are Dhamma-devotees, we are Dhamma-devotees,’ but they are excitable, boisterous, unsteady, mouthy, loose in their talk, muddled in their mindfulness, unalert, unconcentrated, their minds wandering, their senses uncontrolled.
an6.46:3.3 | S: Why do they practice discernment of principles? In what way do they practice discernment of principles? How do they practice discernment of principles?’ | T: Why, indeed, are they Dhamma devotees? For what purpose are they Dhamma devotees? How are they Dhamma devotees?’
an6.46:3.4 | S: In this case the mendicants who practice absorption are not inspired, and the mendicants who practice discernment of principles are not inspired. And they’re not acting for the welfare and happiness of the people, for the people, for the benefit, welfare, and happiness of gods and humans. | T: In that, the jhāna monks do not shine brightly, and the Dhamma-devotee monks do not shine brightly. That is not practicing for the welfare of the masses, for the happiness of the masses, for the good of the masses, nor for the welfare & happiness of devas & human beings.
an6.46:4.1 | S: Now, take a case where mendicants who practice discernment of principles praise only others like them, not mendicants who practice absorption. | T: “And further, there is the case where Dhamma-devotee monks praise only Dhamma-devotee monks, and not jhāna monks.
an6.46:4.2 | S: In this case the mendicants who practice discernment of principles are not inspired, and the mendicants who practice absorption are not inspired. And they’re not acting for the welfare and happiness of the people, for the people, for the benefit, welfare, and happiness of gods and humans. | T: In that, the Dhamma-devotee monks do not shine brightly, and the jhāna monks do not shine brightly. That is not practicing for the welfare of the masses, for the happiness of the masses, for the good of the masses, nor for the welfare & happiness of devas & human beings.
an6.46:5.1 | S: And take a case where mendicants who practice absorption praise only others like them, not mendicants who practice discernment of principles. | T: “And further, there is the case where jhāna monks praise only jhāna monks, and not Dhamma-devotee monks.
an6.46:5.2 | S: In this case the mendicants who practice absorption are not inspired, and the mendicants who practice discernment of principles are not inspired. And they’re not acting for the welfare and happiness of the people, for the people, for the benefit, welfare, and happiness of gods and humans. | T: In that, the jhāna monks do not shine brightly, and the Dhamma-devotee monks do not shine brightly. That is not practicing for the welfare of the masses, for the happiness of the masses, for the good of the masses, nor for the welfare & happiness of devas & human beings.
an6.46:6.1 | S: So you should train like this: | T: “Thus, friends, you should train yourselves:
an6.46:6.2 | S: ‘As mendicants who practice discernment of principles, we will praise mendicants who practice absorption.’ | T: ‘Being Dhamma-devotee monks, we will speak in praise of jhāna monks.’
an6.46:6.3 | S: That’s how you should train. | T: That’s how you should train yourselves.
an6.46:6.4 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an6.46:6.5 | S: Because it’s rare to find such incredible individuals in the world who have direct meditative experience of the element free of death. | T: Because these are amazing people, hard to find in the world: those who dwell touching the deathless element with the body.
an6.46:7.1 | S: So you should train like this: | T: “And thus, friends, you should train yourselves:
an6.46:7.2 | S: ‘As mendicants who practice absorption, we will praise mendicants who practice discernment of principles.’ | T: ‘Being jhāna monks, we will speak in praise of Dhamma-devotee monks.’
an6.46:7.3 | S: That’s how you should train. | T: That’s how you should train yourselves.
an6.46:7.4 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an6.46:7.5 | S: Because it’s incredibly rare to find individuals in the world who see the meaning of a deep saying with penetrating wisdom.” | T: Because these are amazing people, hard to find in the world: those who penetrate with discernment statements of profound meaning.”
an6.46:7.6 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## an6.55
an6.55:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
an6.55:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha, on the Vulture’s Peak Mountain. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha on Vulture Peak Mountain.
an6.55:1.3 | S: Now at that time Venerable Soṇa was staying near Rājagaha in the Cool Grove. | T: And on that occasion Ven. Soṇa was staying near Rājagaha in the Cool Forest.
an6.55:1.4 | S: Then as he was in private retreat this thought came to his mind, | T: Then, as Ven. Soṇa was meditating in seclusion [after doing walking meditation until the skin of his soles was split & bleeding], this train of thought arose in his awareness:
an6.55:1.5 | S: “I am one of the Buddha’s most energetic disciples. | T: “Of the Blessed One’s disciples who have aroused their persistence, I am one,
an6.55:1.6 | S: Yet my mind is not freed from defilements by not grasping. But my family has wealth. I could enjoy that wealth and make merit. | T: but my mind is not released from the effluents through lack of clinging/sustenance. Now, my family has enough wealth that it would be possible to enjoy wealth & make merit.
an6.55:1.7 | S: Why don’t I resign the training and return to a lesser life, so I can enjoy my wealth and make merit?” | T: What if I were to disavow the training, return to the lower life, enjoy wealth, & make merit?”
an6.55:2.1 | S: Then the Buddha knew Venerable Soṇa’s train of thought. As easily as a strong person would extend or contract their arm, he vanished from the Vulture’s Peak and reappeared in the Cool Grove in front of Soṇa, | T: Then the Blessed One, as soon as he perceived with his awareness the train of thought in Ven. Soṇa’s awareness, disappeared from Vulture Peak Mountain—just as a strong man might extend his flexed arm or flex his extended arm—appeared in the Cool Forest right in front of Ven. Soṇa,
an6.55:2.2 | S: and sat down on the seat spread out. | T: and sat down on a prepared seat.
an6.55:2.3 | S: Soṇa bowed to the Buddha and sat down to one side. | T: Ven. Soṇa, after bowing down to the Blessed One, sat to one side.
an6.55:2.4 | S: The Buddha said to him, | T: As he was sitting there, the Blessed One said to him,
an6.55:3.1 | S: “Soṇa, as you were in private retreat didn’t this thought come to your mind: | T: “Just now, as you were meditating in seclusion, didn’t this train of thought appear to your awareness:
an6.55:3.2 | S: ‘I am one of the Buddha’s most energetic disciples. | T: ‘Of the Blessed One’s disciples who have aroused their persistence, I am one,
an6.55:3.3 | S: Yet my mind is not freed from defilements by not grasping. But my family has wealth. I could enjoy that wealth and make merit. | T: but my mind is not released from the effluents.…
an6.55:3.4 | S: Why don’t I resign the training and return to a lesser life, so I can enjoy my wealth and make merit?’” | T: What if I were to disavow the training, return to the lower life, enjoy wealth, & make merit?’”
an6.55:3.5 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, lord.”
an6.55:4.1 | S: “What do you think, Soṇa? | T: “Now what do you think, Soṇa?
an6.55:4.2 | S: When you were still a layman, weren’t you a good player of the arched harp?” | T: Before, when you were a house-dweller, were you skilled at playing the vīṇā?”
an6.55:4.3 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, lord.”
an6.55:4.4 | S: “When your harp’s strings were tuned too tight, was it resonant and playable?” | T: “And what do you think? When the strings of your vīṇā were too taut, was your vīṇā in tune & playable?”
an6.55:4.5 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, lord.”
an6.55:5.1 | S: “When your harp’s strings were tuned too slack, was it resonant and playable?” | T: “And what do you think? When the strings of your vīṇā were too loose, was your vīṇā in tune & playable?”
an6.55:5.2 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, lord.”
an6.55:6.1 | S: “But when your harp’s strings were tuned neither too tight nor too slack, but fixed at an even tension, was it resonant and playable?” | T: “And what do you think? When the strings of your vīṇā were neither too taut nor too loose, but tuned [literally: established] to be right on pitch, was your vīṇā in tune & playable?”
an6.55:6.2 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, lord.”
an6.55:7.1 | S: “In the same way, Soṇa, when energy is too forceful it leads to restlessness. When energy is too slack it leads to laziness. | T: “In the same way, Soṇa, over-aroused persistence leads to restlessness, overly slack persistence leads to laziness.
an6.55:7.2 | S: So, Soṇa, you should focus on energy and serenity, find a balance of the faculties, and learn the character of this situation.” | T: Thus you should determine the right pitch for your persistence, attune [‘penetrate,’ ‘ferret out’] the pitch of the (five) faculties (to that), and there pick up your theme.”
an6.55:7.3 | S: “Yes, sir,” Soṇa replied. | T: “Yes, lord,” Ven. Soṇa answered the Blessed One.
an6.55:7.4 | S: After advising Soṇa like this, the Buddha, as easily as a strong person would extend or contract their arm, vanished from the Cool Grove and reappeared on the Vulture’s Peak. | T: Then, having given this exhortation to Ven. Soṇa, the Blessed One—as a strong man might extend his flexed arm or flex his extended arm—disappeared from the Cool Forest and appeared on Vulture Peak Mountain.
an6.55:8.1 | S: After some time Soṇa focused on energy and serenity, found a balance of the faculties, and learned the pattern of this situation. | T: So after that, Ven. Soṇa determined the right pitch for his persistence, attuned the pitch of the (five) faculties (to that), and there picked up his theme.

