an6.55:8.2 | S: Then Soṇa, living alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute, soon realized the supreme culmination of the spiritual path in this very life. He lived having achieved with his own insight the goal for which gentlemen rightly go forth from the lay life to homelessness. | T: Dwelling alone, secluded, heedful, ardent, & resolute, he in no long time reached & remained in the supreme goal of the holy life for which clansmen rightly go forth from home into homelessness, directly knowing & realizing it for himself in the here & now.
an6.55:8.3 | S: He understood: “Rebirth is ended; the spiritual journey has been completed; what had to be done has been done; there is nothing further for this place.” | T: He knew: “Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world.”
an6.55:8.4 | S: And Venerable Soṇa became one of the perfected. | T: And thus Ven. Soṇa became another one of the arahants.
an6.55:9.1 | S: Then, when Soṇa had attained perfection, he thought, | T: Then, on the attainment of arahantship, this thought occurred to Ven. Soṇa:
an6.55:9.2 | S: “Why don’t I go to the Buddha and declare my enlightenment in his presence?” | T: “What if I were to go to the Blessed One and, on arrival, to declare gnosis in his presence?”
an6.55:9.3 | S: Then Soṇa went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: So he then went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One:
an6.55:10.1 | S: “Sir, a mendicant who is perfected—with defilements ended, who has completed the spiritual journey, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, achieved their heart’s goal, utterly ended the fetter of continued existence, and is rightly freed through enlightenment—is dedicated to six things. | T: “When a monk is an arahant, with his effluents ended, one who has reached fulfillment, done the task, laid down the burden, attained the true goal, totally destroyed the fetter of becoming, and is released through right gnosis, he is dedicated to six things:
an6.55:10.2 | S: They are dedicated to renunciation, seclusion, kindness, the ending of craving, the ending of grasping, and mental clarity. | T: renunciation, seclusion, non-afflictiveness, the ending of craving, the ending of clinging/sustenance, & non-deludedness.
an6.55:11.1 | S: It may be, sir, that one of the venerables here thinks: | T: “Now it may occur to a certain venerable one to think,
an6.55:11.2 | S: ‘Maybe this venerable is dedicated to renunciation solely out of mere faith.’ | T: ‘Perhaps it is entirely dependent on conviction that this venerable one is dedicated to renunciation,’
an6.55:11.3 | S: But it should not be seen like this. | T: but it should not be seen in that way.
an6.55:11.4 | S: A mendicant with defilements ended does not see in themselves anything more to do, or anything that needs improvement. They’re dedicated to renunciation because they’re free of greed, hate, and delusion with the end of greed, hate, and delusion. | T: The monk whose effluents are ended, having fulfilled (the holy life), does not see in himself anything further to do, or anything further to add to what he has done. It is because of the ending of passion, because of his being free of passion, that he is dedicated to renunciation. It is because of the ending of aversion, because of his being free of aversion, that he is dedicated to renunciation. It is because of the ending of delusion, because of his being free of delusion, that he is dedicated to renunciation.
an6.55:12.1 | S: It may be, sir, that one of the venerables here thinks: | T: “Now it may occur to a certain venerable one to think,
an6.55:12.2 | S: ‘Maybe this venerable is dedicated to seclusion because they enjoy possessions, honor, and popularity.’ | T: ‘Perhaps it is because he desires gain, honor, & fame that this venerable one is dedicated to seclusion’…
an6.55:12.3 | S: But it should not be seen like this. … | T: (none)
an6.55:12.4 | P: Khīṇāsavo, bhante, bhikkhu vusitavā katakaraṇīyo karaṇīyaṁ attano asamanupassanto katassa vā paṭicayaṁ khayā rāgassa vītarāgattā pavivekādhimutto hoti, khayā dosassa vītadosattā pavivekādhimutto hoti, khayā mohassa vītamohattā pavivekādhimutto hoti. | T: (none)
an6.55:13.1 | S: It may be, sir, that one of the venerables here thinks: | T: (none)
an6.55:13.2 | S: ‘Maybe this venerable is dedicated to kindness because they believe that adhering to precepts and observances is essential.’ | T: ‘Perhaps it is because he falls back on attachment to habits & practices as being essential that he is dedicated to non-afflictiveness,’
an6.55:13.3 | S: But it should not be seen like this. … | T: but it should not be seen in that way.
an6.55:13.4 | P: Khīṇāsavo, bhante, bhikkhu vusitavā katakaraṇīyo karaṇīyaṁ attano asamanupassanto katassa vā paṭicayaṁ khayā rāgassa vītarāgattā abyāpajjādhimutto hoti, khayā dosassa vītadosattā abyāpajjādhimutto hoti, khayā mohassa vītamohattā abyāpajjādhimutto hoti. | T: The monk whose effluents are ended, having fulfilled (the holy life), does not see in himself anything further to do, or anything further to add to what he has done. It is because of the ending of passion, because of his being free of passion, that he is dedicated to non-afflictiveness. It is because of the ending of aversion, because of his being free of aversion, that he is dedicated to non-afflictiveness. It is because of the ending of delusion, because of his being free of delusion, that he is dedicated to non-afflictiveness.
an6.55:14.1 | S: They’re dedicated to the ending of craving because they’re free of greed, hate, and delusion with the end of greed, hate, and delusion. | T: “It is because of the ending of passion, because of his being free of passion… because of the ending of aversion, because of his being free of aversion… because of the ending of delusion, because of his being free of delusion, that he is dedicated to the ending of craving…
an6.55:15.1 | S: They’re dedicated to the ending of grasping because they’re free of greed, hate, and delusion with the end of greed, hate, and delusion. | T: to the ending of clinging/sustenance…
an6.55:16.1 | S: They’re dedicated to clarity of mind because they’re free of greed, hate, and delusion with the end of greed, hate, and delusion. | T: to non-deludedness.
an6.55:17.1 | S: When a mendicant’s mind is rightly freed like this, even if compelling sights come into the range of vision they don’t overcome their mind. | T: “Even if powerful forms cognizable by the eye come into the visual range of a monk whose mind is thus rightly released, his mind is neither overpowered nor even engaged.
an6.55:17.2 | S: Their mind is quite untainted. It is steady, imperturbable, observing disappearance. | T: Being still, having reached imperturbability, he focuses on their passing away.
an6.55:17.3 | S: Even if compelling sounds … | T: And even if powerful sounds…
an6.55:17.4 | S: smells … | T: aromas…
an6.55:17.5 | S: tastes … | T: flavors…
an6.55:17.6 | S: touches … | T: tactile sensations.…
an6.55:17.7 | S: and ideas come into the range of the mind they don’t overcome the mind. | T: Even if powerful ideas cognizable by the intellect come into the mental range of a monk whose mind is thus rightly released, his mind is neither overpowered nor even engaged.
an6.55:17.8 | S: Their mind is quite untainted. It is steady, imperturbable, observing disappearance. | T: Being still, having reached imperturbability, he focuses on their passing away.
an6.55:17.9 | S: Suppose there was a mountain that was one solid mass of rock, without cracks or holes. | T: “Just as if there were a mountain of rock—without cracks, without fissures, one solid mass—
an6.55:17.10 | S: Even if violent storms were to blow up out of the east, the west, the north, and the south, | T: and then from the east there were to come a powerful storm of wind & rain: The mountain would neither shiver nor quiver nor shake. And then from the west… the north… the south there were to come a powerful storm of wind & rain:
an6.55:17.11 | P: atha uttarāya cepi disāya āgaccheyya bhusā vātavuṭṭhi … | T: (none)
an6.55:17.12 | S: they couldn’t make it shake or rock or tremble. | T: The mountain would neither shiver nor quiver nor shake.
an6.55:17.13 | S: In the same way, when a mendicant’s mind is rightly freed like this, even if compelling sights come into the range of vision they don’t overcome their mind. … | T: In the same way, even if powerful forms cognizable by the eye come into the visual range of a monk whose mind is thus rightly released, his mind is neither overpowered nor even engaged.
an6.55:17.14 | P: Amissīkatamevassa cittaṁ hoti, ṭhitaṁ āneñjappattaṁ vayañcassānupassati. | T: Being still, having reached imperturbability, he focuses on their passing away.
an6.55:17.15 | P: Bhusā cepi sotaviññeyyā saddā … | T: And even if powerful sounds… aromas… flavors… tactile sensations.…
an6.55:17.16 | P: ghānaviññeyyā gandhā … | T: (none)
an6.55:17.17 | P: jivhāviññeyyā rasā … | T: (none)
an6.55:17.18 | P: kāyaviññeyyā phoṭṭhabbā … | T: (none)
an6.55:17.19 | P: manoviññeyyā dhammā manassa āpāthaṁ āgacchanti, nevassa cittaṁ pariyādiyanti. | T: Even if powerful ideas cognizable by the intellect come into the mental range of a monk whose mind is thus rightly released, his mind is neither overpowered nor even engaged.
