## thag13.1
thag13.1:1.1 | S: He who was special in the kingdom, | T: Who once was highly exalted in the state,
thag13.1:1.2 | S: the footman to the king of Aṅga, | T: having gone into the service of the king of Aṅga,
thag13.1:1.3 | S: today is special in the Dhamma— | T: today is exalted in qualities:
thag13.1:1.4 | S: Soṇa has gone beyond suffering. | T: Soṇa, gone beyond suffering.
thag13.1:2.1 | S: Five to cut, five to drop, | T: Five should be cut, five abandoned,
thag13.1:2.2 | S: and five more to develop. | T: and five above all developed.
thag13.1:2.3 | S: When a mendicant slips five chains | T: Having gone past five bonds,
thag13.1:2.4 | S: they’re said to have crossed the flood. | T: a monk is said to be one who’s crossed over.
thag13.1:3.1 | S: If a monk is insolent and negligent, | T: For a monk who is insolent, heedless,
thag13.1:3.2 | S: concerned only with externals, | T: wishing for what’s outside,
thag13.1:3.3 | S: their ethics, immersion, and wisdom | T: virtue, concentration, & discernment
thag13.1:3.4 | S: do not become fulfilled. | T: don’t go to completion,
thag13.1:4.1 | S: They dump what should be done, | T: because what should be done is cast aside,
thag13.1:4.2 | S: and do what should not be done. | T: what shouldn’t be done is done.
thag13.1:4.3 | S: For the insolent and the negligent, | T: For those who are insolent, heedless,
thag13.1:4.4 | S: their defilements only grow. | T: their effluents increase.
thag13.1:5.1 | S: Those that have properly undertaken | T: But those whose mindfulness immersed in the body is constantly well-undertaken,
thag13.1:5.2 | S: constant mindfulness of the body, | T: (none)
thag13.1:5.3 | S: don’t cultivate what should not be done, | T: —who don’t engage in what shouldn’t be done,
thag13.1:5.4 | S: but always do what should be done. | T: who persevere in what should be done,
thag13.1:5.5 | S: Mindful and aware, | T: mindful, alert—
thag13.1:5.6 | S: their defilements come to an end. | T: their effluents go to their end.
thag13.1:6.1 | S: The straight path has been explained— | T: Go by the straight path pointed out,
thag13.1:6.2 | S: go on it and don’t turn back. | T: don’t turn back.
thag13.1:6.3 | S: Urge yourself on | T: You yourself should reprove yourself
thag13.1:6.4 | S: and take yourself to extinguishment. | T: should advance to unbinding.
thag13.1:7.1 | S: When my energy was over-exerted, | T: When I overexerted my effort,
thag13.1:7.2 | S: the supreme Teacher in the world | T: the Teacher unexcelled in the world, the One with Eyes,
thag13.1:7.3 | S: created the simile of the arched harp for me. | T: making the lute simile,
thag13.1:7.4 | S: The Clear-eyed One taught the Dhamma, | T: taught me the Dhamma.
thag13.1:7.5 | S: and when I heard what he said, | T: I, having heard his words,
thag13.1:7.6 | S: I happily did his bidding. | T: dwelled delighting in his bidding.
thag13.1:8.1 | S: Practicing serenity of mind | T: I practiced in tune,
thag13.1:8.2 | S: for the attainment of the highest goal. | T: attaining the foremost goal.
thag13.1:8.3 | S: I’ve attained the three knowledges | T: The three knowledges are attained.
thag13.1:8.4 | S: and fulfilled the Buddha’s instructions. | T: The Buddha’s bidding, done.
thag13.1:9.1 | S: When you’re dedicated to renunciation | T: When one’s awareness is dedicated to renunciation,
thag13.1:9.2 | S: and seclusion of the heart; | T: seclusion,
thag13.1:9.3 | S: when you’re dedicated to kindness | T: non-afflictiveness,
thag13.1:9.4 | S: and the end of grasping; | T: the ending of clinging,
thag13.1:10.1 | S: when you’re dedicated to the ending of craving | T: the ending of craving,
thag13.1:10.2 | S: and clarity of heart; | T: & non-deludedness,
thag13.1:10.3 | S: and you’ve seen the arising of the senses, | T: seeing the arising of the sense media,
thag13.1:10.4 | S: your mind is rightly freed. | T: the mind is rightly released.
thag13.1:11.1 | S: For that one, rightly freed, | T: For that monk, rightly released,
thag13.1:11.2 | S: a mendicant with peaceful mind, | T: his heart at peace.
thag13.1:11.3 | S: there’s nothing to be improved, | T: There’s nothing to be done,
thag13.1:11.4 | S: and nothing more to do. | T: nothing to add to what’s done.
thag13.1:12.1 | S: As the wind cannot stir | T: As a single mass of rock isn’t moved by the wind,
thag13.1:12.2 | S: a solid mass of rock, | T: (none)
thag13.1:12.3 | S: so too sights, tastes, sounds, | T: even so all forms, flavors, sounds,
thag13.1:12.4 | S: smells, and touches—the lot— | T: aromas, contacts,
thag13.1:13.1 | S: and ideas, whether liked or disliked, | T: ideas desirable & not,
thag13.1:13.2 | S: don’t disturb the unaffected one. | T: have no effect on one who is Such.
thag13.1:13.3 | S: Their mind is steady and unfettered | T: The mind —still, totally released—
thag13.1:13.4 | S: as they observe fall. | T: focuses on their passing away.
thag13.1:13.5 | P: … Soṇo koḷiviso thero … | T: (none)
thag13.1:13.6 | P: Terasanipāto niṭṭhito. | T: (none)
thag13.1:14.0 | P: Tatruddānaṁ | T: (none)
thag13.1:14.1 | P: Soṇo koḷiviso thero, | T: (none)
thag13.1:14.2 | P: ekoyeva mahiddhiko; | T: (none)
thag13.1:14.3 | P: Terasamhi nipātamhi, | T: (none)
thag13.1:14.4 | P: gāthāyo cettha terasāti. | T: (none)


